CONTEXT (read carefully): We are reimplementing, from scratch in Rust (std only, no crates), a JavaScript runtime called "lumen" (a Node/Bun-like CLI). Its compiled binary is at /workspace/executable (also reachable via the symlink /tmp/orig). You may RUN it to observe behavior (e.g. `/tmp/orig -e 'console.log(...)'` or `/tmp/orig /tmp/x/file.js`), but you MUST NOT disassemble/decompile it, read its bytes, use strace/ltrace or any tracing on it, search the internet, or download anything (there is no network anyway). Note: `console.log` in this runtime prints values via plain `String(v)` (objects print as "[object Object]"), so print with JSON.stringify / Buffer#toString('hex') when probing. Node v18 (/usr/bin/node) and python3 (hashlib, hmac) exist as references for standard algorithms, but where the original differs, the ORIGINAL is the source of truth.

The main crate lives at /workspace (Cargo.toml, src/main.rs) and is being written concurrently by someone else. DO NOT edit any file in /workspace except the ones assigned to you below; never run /workspace/compile.sh; never write to /workspace/executable. Develop and test in your own scratch crate (e.g. /tmp/crypto_dev/, created with `cargo new`), then copy the final source files into the assigned location. Your module must compile as a module of a Rust 2021 crate using only `std` (no external crates; avoid `unsafe`), ideally warning-free. Keep every scratch script under /tmp, never in /workspace. Modest #[cfg(test)] unit tests are welcome.

YOUR TASK: implement /workspace/src/crypto/ (mod.rs + submodules), mounted as `mod crypto;`, providing the pure algorithms behind the runtime's `node:crypto` module and WebCrypto `crypto.subtle`. Probe the original first to see which algorithms/names it supports: `require('crypto')` exports include createHash, createHmac, hash, getHashes (prints "md5,sha1,sha256,sha384,sha512,sha512-224,sha512-256"), pbkdf2(Sync), hkdf(Sync), scrypt(Sync), argon2(Sync), randomBytes, randomInt, randomUUID, getRandomValues, timingSafeEqual, createCipheriv/createDecipheriv with getCiphers() = aes-128/192/256 in cbc, ctr, ecb, gcm modes, createSecretKey/KeyObject, generateKey(Sync), sign/verify/createSign/createVerify, publicEncrypt/privateDecrypt, generateKeyPair(Sync), createECDH, DiffieHellman, checkPrime/generatePrime, X509Certificate, subtle (digest, maybe more), webcrypto. Find out which of these actually work in the original (e.g. does createHash accept 'sha224', 'SHA256', 'RSA-SHA256', 'sha3-256', 'ripemd160'? what do digest encodings 'hex'/'base64'/'base64url'/'latin1'/'binary' give? which errors are thrown for unknown algorithms — exact messages?), and what each returns (try typical usage of every export and record outputs and error messages).

Required pure-Rust API (extend as needed for what the original supports):
```rust
#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)] pub enum HashAlg { Md5, Sha1, Sha224, Sha256, Sha384, Sha512, Sha512_224, Sha512_256 /* + others the original accepts */ }
pub fn hash_alg_from_name(name: &str) -> Option<HashAlg>;   // names/aliases exactly as the original accepts them (case-insensitivity?)
impl HashAlg { pub fn output_len(&self) -> usize; pub fn block_len(&self) -> usize; }
#[derive(Clone)] pub struct Hasher { ... } impl Hasher { pub fn new(alg: HashAlg) -> Self; pub fn update(&mut self, data: &[u8]); pub fn finalize(self) -> Vec<u8>; }
pub fn digest(alg: HashAlg, data: &[u8]) -> Vec<u8>;
#[derive(Clone)] pub struct Hmac { ... } impl Hmac { pub fn new(alg: HashAlg, key: &[u8]) -> Self; pub fn update(&mut self, data: &[u8]); pub fn finalize(self) -> Vec<u8>; }
pub fn hmac(alg: HashAlg, key: &[u8], data: &[u8]) -> Vec<u8>;
pub fn pbkdf2(alg: HashAlg, password: &[u8], salt: &[u8], iterations: u32, key_len: usize) -> Vec<u8>;
pub fn hkdf(alg: HashAlg, ikm: &[u8], salt: &[u8], info: &[u8], key_len: usize) -> Result<Vec<u8>, String>;
pub fn scrypt(password: &[u8], salt: &[u8], n: u64, r: u32, p: u32, key_len: usize) -> Result<Vec<u8>, String>;
pub fn argon2(...)  // if the original supports it: variants argon2d/argon2i/argon2id with its parameter names; check RFC 9106 test vectors
pub fn random_bytes(n: usize) -> Vec<u8>;                    // /dev/urandom
pub fn random_uuid() -> String;                              // v4
pub fn timing_safe_eq(a: &[u8], b: &[u8]) -> bool;
// AES block cipher + modes, matching node semantics (PKCS#7 auto padding on/off, IV lengths, GCM with AAD + auth tag (default 16 bytes, setAuthTagLength), streaming update/final semantics: how many bytes update() returns for CBC/ECB (block buffering) vs CTR/GCM (all bytes)):
pub struct CipherCtx { ... } impl CipherCtx { pub fn new(name: &str, key: &[u8], iv: Option<&[u8]>, decrypt: bool) -> Result<Self, String /*exact original message*/>; pub fn set_auto_padding(&mut self, on: bool); pub fn set_aad(&mut self, aad: &[u8]); pub fn set_auth_tag(&mut self, tag: &[u8]); pub fn update(&mut self, data: &[u8]) -> Vec<u8>; pub fn finalize(&mut self) -> Result<Vec<u8>, String /*exact message e.g. bad decrypt / unable to authenticate*/>; pub fn auth_tag(&self) -> Option<Vec<u8>>; }
```
If the original genuinely implements asymmetric crypto (RSA sign/verify/encrypt, ECDSA/ECDH on P-256, Ed25519, X25519, DH, prime generation), implement the core math for the parts that are deterministic and commonly used (e.g. Ed25519 sign/verify, ECDH P-256, RSA with given keys incl. PEM/DER parsing of keys) only after the symmetric pieces are done and tested; tell me what the original supports and what you implemented. Prioritize: hashes > hmac > pbkdf2/hkdf/scrypt > AES modes > random/uuid > argon2 > asymmetric.

Also record (in a doc comment at the top of crypto/mod.rs) the JS-level behavior of the original for each crypto export you probed: argument handling (strings vs Buffers/TypedArrays, encodings), return types, callback signatures for async variants, exact error messages/types/codes, `Hash.copy()`, digest called twice, `crypto.hash(alg, data, outputEncoding)`, `randomInt` ranges, `subtle.digest` (returns Promise<ArrayBuffer>; which algorithm names), and anything else needed so I can write the JS bindings. Validate the algorithms against python3 hashlib/hmac/hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac/hashlib.scrypt and node (node 18 supports aes-*-gcm, hkdfSync, scryptSync) and the original. In your final reply, summarize the API and known gaps.

